  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Thunderstorms return to Lemhi County, Idaho, and southwest\n  Montana with the weather pattern during weekend, bringing \n  lightning and brief, erratic wind gusts\n\n- Warming trend Tuesday through Thursday\n\n- Cooler and showery conditions expected next weekend\n\nA disturbance passing to the south of our region will interact\nwith another more northern disturbance that will drop out of \nBritish Columbia. Shower and thunderstorm activity is likely \nacross northwest Montana this afternoon and evening, along with \nongoing shower and thunderstorms across southwest Montana and \nsoutheast Idaho. In addition, a few storms could develop as far \nnorth as Missoula and up to the Seeley Lake/Ovando region. Any \nstorms that occur today will be capable of producing gusty erratic\nwinds, lightning, and brief heavy rain. The shower activity is \nexpected to diminish after sunset this evening. \n\nThe British Columbia disturbance will continue to track along the\nCanadian border on Sunday, keeping the chance for wrap-around \nshower activity across northwest Montana. It will also bring in \ncooler air, causing temperatures to be near seasonal averages for \nSunday and Monday.\n\nA ridge of high pressure will be building over the region Tuesday\nthrough Thursday. Temperatures will be steadily climbing each day\nwith Wednesday and Thursday being the warmest days so far this \nyear. Most valley locations should be in the upper 80s to low 90s \nby Wednesday afternoon, with the lower elevation valleys of north\ncentral Idaho breaking 100 degrees.\n\nThe vast majority (over 75%) of the long term models are showing \na trough of low pressure moving into the Pacific Northwest by next\nweekend, causing cooler conditions along with widespread shower \nactivity.",
